WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.450
Star Wars fans might best recognize Domhnall
Gleeson as the sneaky General Hux who helps

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:10.120
lead up the First Order, but the Irish actor
has actually assembled an impressive resume

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.929
over the last few years.

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:17.210
From sci-fi extravaganzas to gritty period
pictures, here's what you might've seen Domhnall

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.350
Gleeson starring in before he ventured out
into a galaxy far, far away.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.919
Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows

00:00:23.919 --> 00:00:27.900
Star Wars wasn't Domhnall Gleeson's first
foray into franchise land.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.669
He also enjoyed a brief stint in the final
Harry Potter films as the eldest Weasley kid,

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:37.699
Bill, who's come back from working overseas
to marry Fleur Delacour and take a nice desk

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:40.339
job before the Second Wizarding War erupts.

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:44.570
Although Gleeson has joked that his red hair
was the real reason he got the gig, his ruby

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.120
locks had very little to do with the spell
he'd cast over audiences.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.530
Never Let Me Go

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.179
Although he'd been working for about a decade
before Never Let Me Go came out in 2010, the

00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:59.940
dystopian drama about a group of teen clones
who have been bred to serve as organ donors

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.399
helped launch Gleeson into the mainstream.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:06.689
His role was relatively brief in the picture,
but sharing the screen with an all-star cast

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:11.270
that included Keira Knightley, Andrew Garfield,
Carey Mulligan and Ella Purnell certainly

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.270
didn't hurt.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:16.650
More importantly, the film connected him with
screenwriter Alex Garland, whom he would later

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.730
work with again on several other projects.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:20.770
True Grit

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:26.090
In the Coen Brothers' acclaimed western adaptation,
Gleeson played Moon, a soft-spoken outlaw

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.920
questioned by Jeff Bridges' U.S. Marshal.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.479
The character meets a rather grisly end after
answering some questions for Bridges' lawman,

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:36.960
but while his role isn't big, it's certainly
memorable.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.240
And Gleeson was happy to accept such a brief
part in the picture because, as he put it,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.670
he'd "rather do one day on a really cool movie
than six months on something crap."

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.780
Dredd

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.850
Gleeson continued his trend of small roles
in good movies when he popped up in the cult

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.329
hit Dredd as the Clan Techie in the Peach
Trees slum block.

00:01:56.329 --> 00:02:00.220
He's the grungy computer kid who's running
the security cameras and communications, but

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:05.820
it's understandable if you may not have recognized
Gleeson buried under all that grime.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.179
Black Mirror

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:12.140
One of Gleeson's most heart-wrenching roles
came courtesy of the acclaimed sci-fi anthology

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:17.220
series Black Mirror with the episode "Be Right
Back," which co-starred Hayley Atwell.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.360
The story follows a woman grieving the sudden
death of her boyfriend after he's killed in

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.550
a car crash.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.200
Gleeson offers a haunting performance as the
boyfriend—and then an A.I.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.300
reconstruction of the young man's personality
designed to help Atwell's character grieve

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.450
his passing.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:37.250
The two stars' work made the episode one of
the most acclaimed in the anthology series,

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.720
and Gleeson said he loved the ambition of
the segment, noting it asks some "really interesting

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.180
questions in a way that some of [his] favorite
films ask really interesting questions."

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.180
About Time

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.950
Trading in the supporting roles for a leading
man gig, Gleeson headlined the romantic comedy

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.730
About Time alongside Rachel McAdams.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.260
The story focuses on a man who learns he has
the ability to travel back in time, which

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.870
he uses to woo the love of his life.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:08.120
With the film marking one of Gleeson's first
starring roles in a major production, he said

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.740
he had to get used to being in pretty much
every scene, saying, "There is something wonderful

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.040
about turning up every day and knowing that
not just the character, but the whole movie

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.470
kind of rests on your shoulders."

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.780
Ex Machina

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:26.010
One of Gleeson's most buzzy roles came in
the daring sci-fi flick Ex Machina, where

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.580
he played a low-level tech employee tasked
with "testing" a new AI and android at a private

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:32.810
research facility.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:37.540
The film reunited the actor with writer-director
Alex Garland, and resulted in one of the most

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.910
acclaimed movies of the year, which only helped
advance Gleeson's career even more.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.350
The Revenant

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:48.660
This powerful period drama included a small
but compelling role for Gleeson, who played

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:55.050
fur trader Andrew Henry alongside Leonardo
DiCaprio's nigh unkillable Hugh Glass.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.400
Gleeson was understandably proud of how The
Revenant turned out, but did admit the shoot

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.050
was exceedingly brutal.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.050
The story takes place in the dead of winter
in the wilderness, and he said that when the

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.920
actors were actually working and shooting,
it wasn't so bad.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.290
Things went downhill during the downtime,
though, as he said, "When you're out there,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.180
in and out of the river, standing around and
not knowing if you're going to get to your

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.729
bit that day, those days suck."

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:20.829
American Made

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:26.150
Gleeson also starred opposite A-lister Tom
Cruise in American Made, a tale inspired by

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:33.080
a real-life pilot recruited by a CIA agent
to fly secret spy missions above Central America.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.699
Gleeson described his character as basically
a "composite of different needs for the script,"

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:41.710
adding that he was glad the filmmakers made
him part of the story's action.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:42.800
The rest

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.229
Gleeson has also starred in a bevy of other
pics, including Frank, Unbroken, Brooklyn,

00:04:47.229 --> 00:04:54.430
and director Darren Aronofsky's divisive psychological
thriller mother!, which Gleeson said spoke

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.949
to his urge to be a "part of something that
shakes people to their core."

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:03.289
Indeed, if there is one common thread tying
his eclectic filmography together, it's that

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:05.919
most of his roles do leave a lasting impression.

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:07.710
Thanks for watching!

